  {
    "episode_id": 95620,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95620.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Oh, sure, gosh. Well, I've been the United States representative for my home, our home on the Central Coast of California since January of 2017. Prior to that, I was in the District Attorney's Office down here in Monterey County for about six years. I was on the gang team, so basically dealt with, unfortunately, gang members who committed some very, very serious crimes. down here in Monterey County throughout Monterey County. Prior to that, I was up at the Alameda County District Attorney's Office in Oakland, California doing general felonies up there. During that time in the District Attorney's Office, I volunteered for the Naval Reserve and then volunteered to be mobilized and employed and served him in Afghanistan from 07 to 08 in operation and during freedom as well. The topic that's in the news definitely read right now, especially with what's going on in Afghanistan. We can get into that later. But, you know, I think pretty much always done something that is basically about, you know, serving and public service. It's not something I had planned to do when I was your age. It's just something that sort of wasn't graded me. And then obviously I continued to act on that in the steps that I took forward. Once again, not knowing exactly what I wanted to do, especially at your age, but definitely took steps in order to take advantage of the opportunities that were presented to me mainly because of my education and where I went to school. and what I did basically to, as my mother said, never let the grass grow under my feet and always go forward. Also at the time, once I got back and prior when I was serving in the Monterey County DA's office, I became very active with community organizations. So volunteered for the Veterans Transition Center, worked on getting a Veterans Treatment Court, worked on getting our first Veterans Cemetery here in Monterey County on the Central Coast. And then also worked with the Monterey Bay National Marine Sanctuary and on the Advisory Council on the Community Foundation and a couple of other boards where I volunteered my time. So once again, basically creating and building the foundation upon which this job is all about, be it using evidence to create policy, using evidence is how you proved your case as a prosecutor to what we do now as a policy creator, basically using evidence to do that. but also having a true understanding of what it means and what it takes to serve this community. And having grown up here, having been educated here, having served here, it provided me with a good foundation upon which I can do my job today. And I'm very lucky that I was able to do that, very lucky that my grandfather immigrated here and raised his family here in which we were raised here. Like I said, having that foundation of understanding what it means to give back and how to give back to this community that we call home was an important foundation that, like I said, I continue to use today as a U.S. representative for the Central Coast.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
