    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95620.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, I think, you know, obviously, when you talk about youth, I think one of the most important things you can talk about is education and how we can continue to make sure education is one, accessible and two, affordable. Right now, we're seeing young men and women graduate college with thousands and tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands in debt. And that really stunts their growth and stunts their movement forward. And yes, stunts their future and their opportunities for their future to be frank. Because with that debt, it sort of limits them to what they can do. in regards to buying a home or having a partner or moving or, you know, are deciding to continue their education or switching jobs. And so it's making sure that we can hopefully get to a point where higher education is affordable and a way we can do that. And one of the ways you're going to see that being done is in this infrastructure package, We're going to look at providing free community college, which I think is instrumental. And I say that because look, man, I went to a community college. I went to Monterey Peninsula College for two years. I was fortunate enough to have that opportunity. And look, I needed that to be frank. When I was in high school, I wasn't as focused as you. I can tell you that much. Well, I was, but I wasn't focused on my academics. I was focused on athletics. And I wasn't as well-rounded as you, Isaac and Wanna. and then you could do both. And so I just kind of focused on athletics, but the MPC gave me that opportunity to pivot from athletics to academics and also work full time, to be honest with you, and allowed me that time, that foundation, that kind of stepping stone that I used to go to then a four-year college after that, or two more years at UC Davis. Fortunately, I was able to then pay my way through college because of my full-time work when I was at MPC. Now look, I know it's easy for me to say, especially back then, you could pay for college back then. It's a little tougher to do considering how expensive they are right now. So beyond free community college, if you do go to a four-year law college, how is it that we can also make that more affordable? And I believe it's basically looking at how we can forgive student loan debt. And that's why I signed on to a couple of bills, pieces of legislation, that basically cancel up to $50,000 in student loan debt, which I think is very important, as well as making it easier to understand, you know, loan forgiveness and the modernization of the loan forgiveness. And I put forward a bill that does that as well, makes it a little bit easier to get to understanding how students can get their loans forgiven, especially when they go into public service. So it really is about affordability of colleges and college education and know that we're not just talking about that in Washington DC. We're actually doing something about it as you're going to see in this infrastructure package with free community colleges, what we're shooting for, but also working to forgive student loan debt up to certain amounts for those who have incurred it. But also then looking at before that, looking at universal pre-K, obviously people understand how important it is. you know, those early years to make sure that basically there is pre-K resources available for those families and those children prior to them taking advantage of our public school system and making that universal as well and that available and accessible as well. So I think basically it's making sure education, education is affordable, education is accessible and know that that's being done in Washington DC.",
