    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95620.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Oh, look, I think, you know, obviously where I'm located on the Central Coast to California and basically the legislation part of it is all the way across the country in Washington, D.C. You know, I travel back and forth and I make that a point because although I work in Washington, D.C., my home and the people I represent are all the way out here on the Central Coast to California. So every week I'm going back and forth. That's important to me. I'm not staying in Washington DC on the weekends. I come back here, yes, to be with my family, but more importantly, well, not more importantly, but also to be with the people of the Central Coast. And so I get out and about. And so if you look at my Go to my webpage and you follow me on Facebook or Twitter. You'll see that when I'm in the district, I'm meeting with all sorts of people. I'm going to high schools. I'm going to elementary schools. I'm going to middle schools. I'm going to daycare centers. I'm going to pretty much everywhere and anywhere with that I can help in order to get that type of information upon what I hear and have the pulse of what's going on in my community. As you know, This 20th congressional district here on the Central Coast is quite big. All of Monterey County, all of San Benito County is significant part of Santa Cruz County and a sliver of Santa Clara County up in Gilroytes, one of the bigger districts in California. And so it takes a lot of drive time, a lot of time out and about. but in getting into all of the different and diverse communities. And that's another great thing about this district is it's very diverse in more ways than one. It's diverse ethnically, it's diverse politically, it's diverse geographically, it's diverse economically. And it's not definitely not a monolithic district as there are in Congress, trust me. But what makes my district special, what makes it unique is the diversity of it. But that also takes making sure you listen to everybody. You get in front of everybody. I mean, look, I think you'll realize, Isaac, that when you look at employment, no matter what you do, 80% of the job is showing up. And so it's my job to show up. Now, I obviously have a little bit different in my job. I just don't go to the office. I go throughout the central coast. The central coast is my office. And so that's where I'm at when I'm here. And it's basically continuing to be in front of the people to most importantly to listen to people and hear what they're saying. And so to answer your question, here in the district, how I stay in tune and have my hand on the pulse, I show up. And I think one of the best things that people say is when they look at my Facebook feed or when they look at me on Twitter, they'll say, man, you're everywhere. And that's right. I am everywhere. That's my job. And so being everywhere allows me to be a better representative of the people who are everywhere in this district. So that's the biggest part about it. Now, look, legislatively, that's a whole other class I could teach on that and how we get stuff done. Once I get these ideas from people, once I know, What needs to be done at the federal level for the people on the central coast? It's then going through the legislative process back in Washington DC And that's not easy I gotta say that's probably one of the the toughest jobs of of my of my job is to pass legislation because as you know, look being in the district and being out and about I get energy from that I like people I'm curious about people so showing up is the easy part for me It's just doing the work that doesn't bother me. I don't mind working hard. That's why I'm here But a passing legislation obviously takes a lot of work, but it takes a lot of finding that balance to be honest with you. As one of my colleagues said, passing legislation is like being a P. We hockey team on a P. We hockey team and playing the professional hockey team, the Olympic hockey team from the Soviet Union back in 1980. and being able to score that puck as the Pee Wee hockey team. So it takes a little bit of skill, a lot of work, and a lot of luck to basically get that puck through those legs and into that net. But you can't just do it by just hitting it. You got to work hard at it, and you got to continue it, and it may not take the first game that you're going to beat the Soviet Union. It may take a few games in which you actually, not just beat them, but actually score on them. And that's kind of what legislation making is about. It takes persistence and it takes continuing to try to find a way, try to find that crack and that crevice upon which you can get that goal and that net and get that legislation passed. So that's pretty tough to do, but it's also fun because you know, once you do that, you're helping the people that mean the most to me as a representative, and that's my community members here on the Central Coast.",
